<!-- 6. SECURITY AND PRIVACY ARCHITECTURE                              -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<heading level="2">Security and Privacy Architecture</heading>

<text>
  SLAKSHNA is built from the ground up to operate securely over untrusted public
  networks, proxies, and shared supercomputers:
</text>

<heading level="3">End-to-End Cryptographic Transport (TLS 1.3 over QUIC)</heading>

<text>
  Every node generates an Ed25519 cryptographic keypair upon startup
  (<code>src/identity.rs</code>). All communication across the Iroh mesh is wrapped
  in end-to-end TLS 1.3 encryption. Public proxy services (Playit.gg) act purely
  as raw packet forwarders — they cannot read, decrypt, or tamper with model
  weights because they do not hold the private keys.
</text>

<heading level="3">Poisoning Defence</heading>

<text>
  When a node receives a peer's delta, <code>ml_engine.py</code> evaluates the
  update against local validation metrics (Cosine Similarity and Validation Loss).
  If a node submits poisoned or erratic updates, its trust score (alpha) drops,
  rendering its weight in the Federated Averaging formula close to 0.0.
  Additionally, peer deltas are validated for NaN/Inf injection and extreme norm
  anomalies (<code>validate_peer_delta</code>, max_allowed_norm=10.0).
</text>

<heading level="3">Differential Privacy against Data Reconstruction</heading>

<text>
  By combining sparsification/compression with differential privacy (L2 norm
  clipping and configurable Gaussian noise injection), raw local dataset samples
  can never be reconstructed by eavesdroppers or peer nodes.
</text>

<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- 7. RUST MODULE REFERENCE                                           -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<heading level="2">Rust Module Reference</heading>

<heading level="3">config — Configuration (src/config.rs)</heading>

<text>
  Loads a per-node TOML file and deserialises it into the following structs:
</text>

<table>
  <ched/>Struct<ched/>Fields<ched/>Purpose<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>Config</code><fcel/>federation, training, node, network, discovery, logging<fcel/>Top-level configuration container<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>FederationConfig</code><fcel/>id, name<fcel/>Identifies the federation; nodes sharing the same id train together<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>TrainingConfig</code><fcel/>epoch_duration_secs (default 600), sync_deadline_secs (default 300), expected_peers (default 6)<fcel/>Federated epoch timing and sync barrier<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>NodeConfig</code><fcel/>id, data_dir, gpu_id<fcel/>Per-node identity, storage path, and GPU assignment<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>NetworkConfig</code><fcel/>host, p2p_port, ws_port, api_port, peers, allowed_peers<fcel/>Network binding and optional seed peers / whitelist<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>DiscoveryConfig</code><fcel/>mdns, dht, dns, relay (all default true)<fcel/>Toggle serverless discovery mechanisms<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>LoggingConfig</code><fcel/>level<fcel/>Log verbosity (debug, info, warn, error)<nl/>
</table>

<heading level="3">identity — Node Identity (src/identity.rs)</heading>

<text>
  Provides cryptographic identity for each node:
</text>

<list>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>NodeId</bold> — bech32-encoded (HRP "slakshna") truncated SHA-256 of the Ed25519 public key. Stable across restarts as long as the data_dir persists.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Keypair</bold> — Ed25519 signing/verifying key pair. Supports generation, serialisation from bytes/hex, signing, verification, and a domain-separated <code>transport_seed()</code> for deriving a stable Iroh EndpointId.</text>
</list>

<heading level="3">history — Update History (src/history.rs)</heading>

<text>
  Maintains a per-peer append-only log of federated learning records:
</text>

<list>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>RecordKind::ModelUpdate</bold> — Contains delta_hash and the base64-encoded compressed_delta.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>RecordKind::PeerReview</bold> — Contains target_node, update_hash, loss_drop, and trust_score.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>UpdateRecord</bold> — Hash-chained (prev_hash → SHA-256 hash) with node_id and signature.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>trust_rankings(k)</bold> — Accumulates trust scores from all PeerReview records and returns the top-k peers.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>COHORT_SIZE</bold> = 4 — Number of most-trusted peers in the aggregation cohort per epoch.</text>
</list>

<heading level="3">state — Persistent State (src/state.rs)</heading>

<text>
  RocksDB-backed storage under <code>{data_dir}/rocksdb</code>:
</text>

<list>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text><code>meta:keypair</code> — 32-byte Ed25519 signing key, generated once and reused.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text><code>meta:round</code> — u64 completed federated epoch counter.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text><code>peer:&lt;EndpointId&gt;</code> — Timestamp of last contact; every peer ever met is stored and redialled on restart.</text>
</list>

<heading level="3">network::mesh — Iroh Mesh Network (src/network/mesh.rs)</heading>

<text>
  The MeshNetwork struct implements the Network trait and manages the entire P2P
  lifecycle:
</text>

<list class="ordered">
  <ldiv><marker>1.</marker></ldiv>
  <text>Derive a deterministic transport SecretKey from the persisted federation keypair.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>2.</marker></ldiv>
  <text>Bind an Iroh Endpoint with configurable mDNS, DHT, DNS, relay, and MemoryLookup discovery.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>3.</marker></ldiv>
  <text>Spawn a Gossip instance with 10 MB max message size, attached to the ALPN-routed protocol Router.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>4.</marker></ldiv>
  <text>Derive the gossip TopicId from SHA-256 of the federation id — membership is entirely configuration-driven.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>5.</marker></ldiv>
  <text>Subscribe to the topic with seed peers (config + RocksDB cache), using <code>subscribe</code> (not subscribe_and_join) so a node starts alone if needed.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>6.</marker></ldiv>
  <text>Run background tasks: mDNS event forwarding, periodic re-join of all remembered peers (every 60s), and the main inbound/outbound select loop.</text>
  <ldiv><marker>7.</marker></ldiv>
  <text>Enforce optional peer whitelisting via <code>allowed_peers</code>.</text>
</list>

<heading level="3">api — HTTP REST and WebSocket API (src/api.rs)</heading>

<table>
  <ched/>Method<ched/>Endpoint<ched/>Description<nl/>
  <fcel/>GET<fcel/><code>/</code><fcel/>Returns system name, version, and available endpoints<nl/>
  <fcel/>GET<fcel/><code>/status</code><fcel/>Returns federation id, completed round, active Iroh peer count, and EndpointId<nl/>
  <fcel/>GET<fcel/><code>/updates</code><fcel/>Returns every model update and peer review on record<nl/>
  <fcel/>GET<fcel/><code>/updates/latest</code><fcel/>Returns the most recent record in the local update history<nl/>
  <fcel/>GET<fcel/><code>/updates/:index</code><fcel/>Returns what each participant contributed at that position in its log<nl/>
  <fcel/>GET<fcel/><code>/leaderboard</code><fcel/>Returns node trust score rankings (alpha / w_i)<nl/>
  <fcel/>GET<fcel/><code>/peers</code><fcel/>Returns connected peers, EndpointId, and all known (cached) peers<nl/>
  <fcel/>WS<fcel/><code>ws://host:ws_port/ws</code><fcel/>Live WebSocket stream emitting peer evaluation updates<nl/>
</table>

<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- 8. ML ENGINE REFERENCE                                             -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<heading level="2">ML Engine Reference (ml_engine.py)</heading>

<heading level="3">Execution Flow</heading>

<text>
  The Rust node spawns <code>python ml_engine.py &lt;my_id&gt; [peer_id ...]</code>
  once per federated epoch. The engine's final stdout line is a JSON object
  conforming to the <code>MLEngineOutput</code> contract:
</text>

<code>
  <label value="JSON"/>
  <content><![CDATA[{
  "weights":          { "<peer_id>": <w_i_float>, ... },
  "model_hash":       "<sha256_hex>",
  "validation_score": <float>,
  "metadata":         "<string>",
  "compressed_delta": "<base64_encoded_sparse_tensor>"
}]]></content>
</code>

<heading level="3">Pipeline Stages</heading>

<list class="ordered">
  <ldiv><marker>1.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>State Initialisation</bold> — Load or create per-node state (alpha weights, grad_alpha, score, round counter). Compute softmax trust weights w_i.</text>

  <ldiv><marker>2.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Bhaskera Config Preparation</bold> — Generate a per-node YAML config from node_template.yaml, overriding dataset paths, checkpoint directories, learning rate, max_steps, Ray ports, and GPU assignment.</text>

  <ldiv><marker>3.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Dataset Tokenisation</bold> — Run <code>bhaskera.launcher.tokenize</code> if no cached parquet files exist. Truncate to 80 rows for fast iteration.</text>

  <ldiv><marker>4.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Distributed Training</bold> — Launch <code>bhaskera.launcher.train</code> via subprocess with real-time stdout parsing for epoch loss tracking. Runs on a private per-node Ray cluster.</text>

  <ldiv><marker>5.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Delta Extraction</bold> — Compute true delta (new_weights − old_weights) for either LoRA adapters (fine-tuning mode) or full parameters (pre-training mode via DCP checkpoints).</text>

  <ldiv><marker>6.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Differential Privacy</bold> — Apply L2 norm clipping (max_norm=100.0) and optional Gaussian noise injection to the delta.</text>

  <ldiv><marker>7.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Error Feedback and SparseLoCo</bold> — Add residual error from the previous epoch, apply Top-K sparsification (sparsity=0.1), save the new residual for the next epoch, and encode to fp16 + base64 for network transmission.</text>

  <ldiv><marker>8.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Peer Delta Aggregation</bold> — Load peer deltas from <code>{data_dir}/network_deltas/{peer_id}_delta.b64</code>, validate (NaN/Inf check, norm threshold), and compute trust-weighted federated average.</text>

  <ldiv><marker>9.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Model Update</bold> — Apply the aggregated delta to the base model (LoRA checkpoint or full-parameter safetensors).</text>

  <ldiv><marker>10.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Peer Evaluation</bold> — Compute cosine similarity between local delta and each peer's delta. Update trust scores with exponential decay (beta=0.1, decay=0.98).</text>
</list>

<heading level="3">Key Functions</heading>

<table>
  <ched/>Function<ched/>Purpose<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>get_device(my_id)</code><fcel/>Map node to a dedicated GPU (CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES or hash-based assignment)<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>prepare_bhaskera_config(my_id, is_malicious, training_mode)</code><fcel/>Generate per-node YAML config with isolated paths, ports, and training hyperparameters<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>apply_differential_privacy_and_clipping(delta_dict, max_norm, noise_multiplier)</code><fcel/>L2 norm clipping and Gaussian noise for DP-SGD<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>validate_peer_delta(delta_dict, max_allowed_norm)</code><fcel/>Reject deltas with NaN/Inf or extreme norms<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>sparsify_tensor(tensor, sparsity)</code><fcel/>SparseLoCo Top-K: keep only the top sparsity fraction of weights<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>flatten_tensors(delta_dict)</code><fcel/>Concatenate all tensors into a single vector for cosine similarity computation<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>get_adapter_path(ckpt_dir)</code><fcel/>Recursively find the most recent safetensors or bin checkpoint file<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>extract_training_metrics(ckpt_dir)</code><fcel/>Read final loss from epoch_loss_tracking.csv and compute perplexity<nl/>
</table>

<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- 9. FEDERATED EPOCH LIFECYCLE                                       -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<heading level="2">Federated Epoch Lifecycle</heading>

<text>
  Each node independently runs a continuous loop in <code>src/main.rs</code>:
</text>

<list class="ordered">
  <ldiv><marker>1.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Clock Synchronisation</bold> — Align to the next wall-clock epoch boundary (<code>epoch_duration_secs</code>).</text>

  <ldiv><marker>2.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Phase A — Local Training and Peer Exchange</bold> — Extract peer deltas from the update history to <code>{data_dir}/network_deltas/</code>, then invoke <code>python ml_engine.py</code>.</text>

  <ldiv><marker>3.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Record and Broadcast</bold> — Append the local ModelUpdate and any PeerReview records to the history, then broadcast each via the gossip swarm.</text>

  <ldiv><marker>4.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Sync Barrier</bold> — Wait until <code>expected_peers</code> updates are collected or <code>sync_deadline_secs</code> elapses.</text>

  <ldiv><marker>5.</marker></ldiv>
  <text><bold>Phase B — Trust Ranking</bold> — Compute the top-trusted cohort (COHORT_SIZE=4) from accumulated peer reviews.</text>
</list>

<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- 10. P2P WIRE PROTOCOL                                              -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<heading level="2">P2P Wire Protocol</heading>

<text>
  All inter-node messages are JSON-serialised and broadcast via
  <code>iroh-gossip</code>. The wire format is defined in
  <code>src/network/mod.rs</code>:
</text>

<table>
  <ched/>Variant<ched/>Fields<ched/>Direction<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>Hello</code><fcel/>node_id<fcel/>Outbound on join<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>Welcome</code><fcel/>node_id, round, peers<fcel/>Reply to Hello<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>GetState</code><fcel/>(none)<fcel/>Request<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>HistorySnapshot</code><fcel/>round, recent_updates<fcel/>Reply to GetState<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>NewUpdate</code><fcel/>UpdateRecord<fcel/>Broadcast (model deltas and peer reviews)<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>GetUpdate</code><fcel/>index<fcel/>Request<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>Ping</code><fcel/>(none)<fcel/>Keepalive<nl/>
  <fcel/><code>Pong</code><fcel/>(none)<fcel/>Reply to Ping<nl/>
</table>

<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- 11. PEER DISCOVERY                                                 -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<heading level="2">Peer Discovery Mechanisms</heading>

<text>
  A node never depends on a particular other node being up. Four independent
  mechanisms find peers, in rough order of speed:
</text>

<table>
  <ched/>Mechanism<ched/>Scope<ched/>Depends on<nl/>
  <fcel/>mDNS (discovery.mdns)<fcel/>Same LAN or same host<fcel/>Nothing — multicast only<nl/>
  <fcel/>Remembered peers<fcel/>Anywhere<fcel/>{data_dir}/rocksdb; every peer ever met is redialled on start<nl/>
  <fcel/>Gossip peer exchange<fcel/>Anywhere<fcel/>One reachable member; the swarm hands over the rest<nl/>
  <fcel/>Mainline DHT / pkarr DNS<fcel/>Internet<fcel/>Public DHT; no server operated by the project<nl/>
</table>

<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- 12. CONFIGURATION REFERENCE                                        -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<heading level="2">TOML Configuration Reference</heading>

<text>
  Every node requires its own <code>.toml</code> configuration file. There is no
  bootstrap node and no node type — membership is decided entirely by the shared
  <code>[federation] id</code>.
</text>

<code>
  <label value="TOML"/>
  <content><![CDATA[[federation]
id = "slakshna-fl-1"        # Nodes sharing this id derive the same gossip topic
name = "SLAKSHNA Federation"

[training]
epoch_duration_secs = 600   # Length of one federated epoch (wall-clock aligned)
sync_deadline_secs = 300    # Wait for peer updates before aggregating
expected_peers = 6          # Release sync barrier early once this many report

[node]
id = "1"
data_dir = "./data-node1"   # Dedicated delta storage directory
gpu_id = 3                  # GPU assigned to this node

[network]
host = "0.0.0.0"
p2p_port = 9000             # Iroh QUIC router listening port
api_port = 8545             # Axum HTTP REST API port
ws_port = 8546              # WebSocket port
peers = []                  # Optional seeds; empty is fine

[discovery]
mdns = true                 # Zero-config LAN discovery
dht = true                  # Serverless global lookup via BitTorrent mainline DHT
dns = true                  # pkarr/DNS lookup
relay = true                # Public relays as fallback transport

[logging]
level = "info"]]></content>
</code>

<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- 13. INSTALLATION AND BUILD                                         -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<heading level="2">Installation and Build</heading>

<heading level="3">Prerequisites</heading>

<list>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text>Rust toolchain (edition 2021) with Cargo</text>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text>Python 3.11+ with pip or uv</text>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text>CMake, clang, libclang-dev (for RocksDB compilation)</text>
  <ldiv><marker>•</marker></ldiv>
  <text>CUDA-capable GPU (optional, CPU fallback available)</text>
</list>

<heading level="3">Build Steps</heading>

<code>
  <label value="Shell"/>
  <content><![CDATA[# 1. Navigate to the Bhaskera ML engine directory
cd Bhaskera

# 2. Run the Bhaskera setup script (installs Python dependencies)
bash setup.sh

# 3. Activate the Bhaskera Python virtual environment
source bhaskera-activate.sh

# 4. Move back to the SLAKSHNA root directory
cd ..

# 5. Run the SLAKSHNA root setup script
bash setup.sh

# 6. Build the Rust P2P node binary in release mode
cargo build --release

# 7. Start your node
./target/release/iiitd --config node1.toml]]></content>
</code>

<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- 14. RUNNING THE SYSTEM                                             -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<heading level="2">Running the System</heading>

<heading level="3">Local (Single Machine, Multiple Nodes)</heading>

<code>
  <label value="Shell"/>
  <content><![CDATA[# Option A: Use the orchestrator
python orchestrator.py
# Enter number of nodes when prompted

# Option B: Start individually (mDNS finds peers on the LAN)
./target/release/iiitd --config node1.toml &
./target/release/iiitd --config node2.toml &
./target/release/iiitd --config node3.toml &]]></content>
</code>

<heading level="3">Cross-City Deployment (via Playit.gg)</heading>

<list class="ordered">
  <ldiv><marker>1.</marker></ldiv>
  <text>Start the Playit tunnel on the main machine: <code>cd ~/playit &amp;&amp; ./playit start</code>. Create a UDP/TCP tunnel pointing to your local p2p_port (e.g. 9000). Note the assigned public endpoint.</text>

  <ldiv><marker>2.</marker></ldiv>
  <text>Start the tunnelled node: <code>./target/release/iiitd --config node1.toml</code>. Note its printed EndpointId.</text>

  <ldiv><marker>3.</marker></ldiv>
  <text>On remote machines, set <code>peers = ["&lt;EndpointId&gt;@&lt;playit_ip&gt;:&lt;playit_port&gt;"]</code> in the TOML config and start.</text>
</list>

<heading level="3">SLURM Supercomputer</heading>

<code>
  <label value="Shell"/>
  <content><![CDATA[# Set gpu_id = 0 in your .toml file (SLURM maps the allocated GPU to index 0)
srun -p gpu --gres=gpu:1 --time=04:00:00 ./target/release/iiitd --config config.toml]]></content>
</code>

<heading level="3">Docker Deployment</heading>

<code>
  <label value="Shell"/>
  <content><![CDATA[# Build and start with Docker Compose
docker compose up -d --build

# Or use the full deploy script (includes SSL, swap, cron)
sudo bash deploy.sh]]></content>
</code>

<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- 15. TESTING MODEL POISONING                                        -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<heading level="2">Testing Model Poisoning and Defence</heading>

<text>
  Simulate a malicious node by setting the <code>MALICIOUS_NODES</code>
  environment variable:
</text>

<code>
  <label value="Shell"/>
  <content><![CDATA[MALICIOUS_NODES="node-2" ./target/release/iiitd --config node2.toml]]></content>
</code>

<text>
  When <code>node-2</code> runs in malicious mode, it injects a destructive
  learning rate (1.0). Other nodes automatically detect negative cosine
  similarity alignment, slash the attacker's trust score, and down-weight its
  updates in the final model aggregation.
</text>

<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- 16. TRUST SCORE MATHEMATICS                                        -->
<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<heading level="2">Trust Score Mathematics</heading>

<text>
  The trust-weighted aggregation follows this formulation:
</text>

<text>
  Given a set of nodes <formula>J</formula>, each node <formula>i</formula>
  maintains raw trust scores <formula>\alpha_j</formula> for every peer
  <formula>j \in J</formula>. Normalised weights are computed as:
</text>

<formula>
  w_j = \frac{e^{\alpha_j}}{\sum_{k \in J} e^{\alpha_k}}
</formula>

<text>
  After each epoch, trust is updated based on the cosine similarity
  <formula>\Delta L_j</formula> between local and peer gradient directions:
</text>

<formula>
  \alpha_j \leftarrow 0.98 \cdot \left( \alpha_j + \beta \cdot \Delta L_j \cdot w_j \cdot (1 - w_j) \right)
</formula>

<text>
  where <formula>\beta = 0.1</formula> is the trust learning rate and
  <formula>0.98</formula> is the exponential decay factor. The aggregated model
  update is:
</text>

<formula>
  \Delta_{\text{agg}} = \sum_{j \in J} w_j \cdot \Delta_j
</formula>

<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
